Comment: 
"@@@ If the index is non-negative and less than the initial size of the vector, then for every index greater than the specified index and less than the size of the vector, the element at that position remains unchanged or continues to be null if it was originally null."

Interpretation: In set method, we replace element at index with new element. The assertion: If index >=0 && index < \old(this.size()) then for all i such that i > index && i < this.size() (which after operation size unchanged) the element at i after operation equals the old element at i (including null). So we need to express that for all int i; index < i && i < this.size(); this.elementAt(i).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i)))? But need to handle null case: "remains unchanged or continues to be null if it was originally null". So we can assert that either both are null or they are equal via equals. Usually we can use (this.elementAt(i) == \old(this.elementAt(i))) || (this.elementAt(i) != null && this.elementAt(i).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i)))) . Simpler: this.elementAt(i) == \old(this.elementAt(i)) || (this.elementAt(i) != null && this.elementAt(i).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i)))) . But maybe we can use .equals with null safe? In Java, calling equals on null throws NPE, so we need to check both null. Could also use Objects.equals(a,b) but not available as observer? Not defined. Use this.elementAt(i) == \old(this.elementAt(i)) || (this.elementAt(i) != null && this.elementAt(i).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i)))) . That ensures unchanged.

We need premise: index >=0 && index < \old(this.size()) . Since size unchanged after set, we can use this.size() as well; but to express initial size we use \old(this.size()). So final assert:

assert (index >= 0 && index < \old(this.size())) => \forall int i; i > index && i < this.size(); (this.elementAt(i) == \old(this.elementAt(i)) || (this.elementAt(i) != null && this.elementAt(i).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i)))));

